findings.descriptionMarkdown:
Resolved. `nesting.rs` now bounds the depth of the syntax tree in cost units and fails closed.

All three gaps from the reattestation are rejected with `the source nests too deeply at line …, column …`, in about 10 ms each (release build, 8 MiB thread):
- 100k-term `+`, `||`, `.length`, `()` and `[0]` chains, in `js` and `ts`;
- 30k nested `(?:` in a regex literal;
- the TypeScript `(0)! / 2 + ((((…` case.

Further bypass attempts were rejected too:
- the original 100k parentheses and unary chains;
- an unterminated string, a regex and a comment each hiding the nesting that follows;
- a hashbang, an HTML comment, U+3000 whitespace and a U+2028 line break before the nesting;
- 1 MB of ambiguous `(1)/1/` division chains and mixed regex/division readings;
- alternating function/class/object/arrow/template nesting.

On a 1 MiB debug thread I binary-searched the deepest source the check accepts for 53 constructs (functions, classes, generators, getters, switch, try, labels, else-if, do-while, destructuring, default parameters, spread, `new`, optional chains, tagged templates, regex classes and lookaheads, and 16 TypeScript type forms). Every deepest accepted source compiled and evaluated without overflowing. A flat 1 MB array literal is accepted and evaluates in 0.3 s, so the pass is not quadratic.

Resolved. Every probe from the original finding now fails with `TypeError: object is not extensible` in the first token; the second token observes nothing. The probes covered:
- tamed `toString`/`hasOwnProperty`;
- the `Promise` constructor and prototype reached through `(async()=>{})()`;
- the iterator-helper and `Iterator.from` wrapper prototypes.

Further probes also passed: the async function constructor, `Array.fromAsync(...).constructor`, `DisposableStack`, array/Map entry iterators, `Iterator.concat`, and the `arguments` prototype. A walk over every global reports all objects frozen. `Promise.prototype.constructor` now throws. Async functions still create promises that never settle, as documented.

Resolved. Each case now reports its authored position:
- the lexical redeclaration: `Identifier `a` has already been declared at line 3, column 6`;
- the strict-mode octal: `… at line 1, column 8`;
- the duplicate regex group: `Invalid regular expression: Duplicated capturing group names at line 1, column 19`.

QuickJS compile errors are now mapped through the locator. Note: the regex validation this repair enabled opened the S1-F1 regex-nesting crash.
